<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="section-6-5">
  <title>Worksheet 6.5 and 6.6, Least‑Squares Problems, Applications to Linear Models</title>

  <page>

    <!-- Exercise 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-6-5-ex-1" workspace="2in"><introduction>
      <p> Fill in the blanks. These questions concern the least‑squares solution <m>\hat{x}</m> to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>.</p>
      
    </introduction><task><statement><p>If <m>A = QR</m>, then <m>A^T A =</m> __________________.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>If the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, then <m>\hat{x} =</m> __________________<m>A^T \vec{b}</m>.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>If <m>\vec{b}</m> is in the column space of <m>A</m>, then <m>A\hat{x} =</m> __________________.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>If <m>A = QR</m> and <m>R</m> is invertible, then <m>\hat{x} =</m> __________________<m>Q^T \vec{b}</m>.</p></statement></task></exercise>

    <!-- Exercise 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-6-5-ex-2" workspace="2in"><introduction>
      <p> These questions concern the least‑squares solution <m>\hat{x}</m> to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>.
      Indicate whether the statements are true or false.</p>
      
    </introduction><task><statement><p>The solution <m>\hat{x}</m> is chosen so that <m>A\hat{x}</m> is as close as possible to <m>\vec{b}</m>.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>If <m>\vec{y} \ne \hat{x}</m>, then <m>\|A\hat{x} - \vec{b}\| &lt; \|A\vec{y} - \vec{b}\|</m>.</p></statement></task><task><statement><p>If the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent, then the least‑squares solution is unique.</p></statement></task></exercise>
  </page>
  <page>

    <!-- Exercise 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-6-5-ex-3" workspace="1.5in"><statement>
      <p> Use the QR decomposition to calculate the least‑squares solution to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>.</p>

      <p>
        <md>
        A = QR =
        \begin{pmatrix}
          \tfrac{2}{3} &amp; -\tfrac{1}{3} \\
          \tfrac{2}{3} &amp;  \tfrac{1}{3} \\
          \tfrac{1}{3} &amp; -\tfrac{2}{3}
        \end{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix}
          3 &amp; 5 \\
          0 &amp; 1
        \end{pmatrix},
        \quad
        \vec{b} =
        \begin{pmatrix}
          7 \\ 3 \\ 1
        \end{pmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement></exercise>


<!-- Exercise 4 -->
    <exercise xml:id="section-6-5-ex-4" workspace="2in"><statement>
      <p> Four points in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> with coordinates <m>(x,y,z)</m> are given in the table below.</p>

      <p>
        <md>
        \begin{array}{c|cccc}
          x &amp; -1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\ \hline
          y &amp;  2 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 \\ \hline
          z &amp;  9 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; -1
        \end{array}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Determine the coefficients <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> for the plane
        <m>z = c_1 x + c_2 y</m> that best fits the data.
        (Hint: normal equations.)
      </p>
    </statement></exercise>


  </page>
</worksheet>